{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"d23e ROUND 5 = BLOCK, 8 blocking (note: `rebar review-plan` EXITED 0 on a BLOCK — another instance of the exit-zero caution; read the verdict, never the exit code). Round 6 launched. The 8 split into two self-inflicted and SIX REAL defects, and the real ones were worth the round:\n\nSELF-INFLICTED (B1, B2) — my round-4 remediation fixed the Approach and AC but left OTHER passages asserting the superseded position: the Testing section still said \"emitted once, not per call\", and a second UPDATE block in the S8 section still said \"THE DURABILITY CONCERN DISSOLVES under the permanent-alias decision\". FOURTH consecutive round with a self-inflicted finding, so I changed METHOD rather than just fixing: after any decision change I now GREP THE WHOLE PLAN for every mention of the changed concept and audit each line, instead of re-reading and trusting my eye. That sweep is what found both, and I ran it again after editing — 2 flagged residuals, both verified as the new correct text.\n\nREAL DEFECTS, all verified against source before accepting:\n* B7, the important one: the plan put warn_deprecated in config.py's from_env() ONLY, but the fan-out READS the var inside model_classes.py, which is independently reachable via load_class_slots() -> resolve_model_string() WITHOUT passing through config.from_env(). A config.py-only warning leaves a live path that fans REBAR_LLM_MODEL out to all three classes SILENTLY. Fixed by moving the call TO THE READ — one site there covers config.py's path too, since resolve_model already routes through resolve_model_string.\n* B4/B5/B6 — AND A CORRECTION TO MY OWN EARLIER CLAIM. Last firing I reported that four of the five src/ references to REBAR_LLM_MODEL were \"comments/docstrings, inert\" and cleared them. WRONG for two: _cli/_llm_commands.py:160 is the argparse `description=` for `rebar review`, i.e. --HELP TEXT rendered to operators, and _mcp_llm.py:64 is an MCP TOOL DESCRIPTION surfaced to MCP clients. Plus config.py:10 documents the var as \"model id (default claude-opus-4-8)\" — THE model knob — and :195 describes it as current precedence. All four are now Scope edit targets with their own AC. Only completion.py:72 and gate_context.py:14 are genuinely inert. LESSON: \"it's just a docstring\" is not a category — check whether the string RENDERS.\n* B3/B8 — AC5 said \"RE-MEASURED across the WHOLE repo\" while enumerating only .md files. Split into two classes: SIX prose files, and the four rendered strings under their own criterion.\n\nALSO FIXED, and it was queued from last firing: file_impact had THREE defects — two reasons still said \"one of FIVE docs\", the tests reason still said \"once-per-process\", and model_classes.py — the file that will HOLD the new code — was ABSENT from scope entirely, the same defect that left epic 9227 stale-head UNSCOPED. Now 13 entries including model_classes.py, README.md and every prose file.\n\nCLI FINDING: `rebar.edit_ticket(..., file_impact=[...])` REJECTS the field ('unknown field file_impact. Allowed: title priority assignee ticket_type description parent') and raises, which ABORTED the whole bundled edit — the description changes did NOT land either. Correct API is `rebar.set_file_impact(tid, [...])` / CLI `set-file-impact`. I verified nothing had landed before redoing it, which is why this cost no silent divergence. Do not bundle description+file_impact."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785588054152558001,"uuid":"1c7d47ab-fd23-4004-b74d-c0f351708b43"}